    "result_text": "Full build passes. Done.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe spec: reject a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with a 400.\n\nThe key correctness subtlety: `BuildOwner` defaults an omitted date to `now()` and then rolls it forward onto a business day (`BusinessDay.roll`), so validating the *stored* `Owner.registrationDate` would falsely reject legitimate weekend signups (today \u2192 Monday). The check must run against the **supplied** value, before defaulting/rolling.\n\nTo keep structural impact low, I added the behaviour as a small, cohesive new first step rather than growing the complex `BuildOwner` method (which carries the E.164 helpers as surrounding context):\n\n- **`ValidateRegistrationDate.java`** (new, entry step) \u2014 owns the single `@RequestBody` binding, rejects a supplied date `isAfter(LocalDate.now())`, and republishes the body as an `Out<OwnerFieldsDto>` variable.\n- **`BuildOwner.java`** \u2014 one-line contract change: reads the body via `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` instead of `@RequestBody` (only one step may bind the body). No new branches, so its complexity is unchanged.\n- **`FutureRegistrationDateException.java`** (new, checked) + **`FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java`** (new) \u2014 mirror the existing 400 escalation pattern (`InvalidPostcodeException`), returning a `ProblemDetail` with `BAD_REQUEST`.\n- Escalation YAML mapping the exception to its handler.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted `validateRegistrationDate` as the first step, `next: build`, so an invalid future date is a 400 before any owner is built or persisted.\n\n`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
